%I A398298 #28 Aug 13 2026 19:57:11
%S A398298 1,2,3,5,6,9,10,12,11,13,19,21,22,25,26,37,38,41,42,44,50,52,43,45,51,
%T A398298 53,54,75,77,83,85,86,89,90,101,102,105,106,108,147,149,150,153,154,
%U A398298 165,166,169,170,172,178,180,201,202,204,210,212,155,171,173,179
%N A398298 Irregular triangle read by rows, where row n lists the numbers whose binary representation forms a valid row/column on a 2*n X 2*n Takuzu board.
%C A398298 Takuzu (also known as Binairo) is a Sudoku-like puzzle on a 2*n X 2*n board that is solved by adding zeros and ones abiding the following rules:
%C A398298 1. Every row and every column has the same number (n) of zeros and ones.
%C A398298 2. A row or column cannot have more than two consecutive zeros or ones.
%C A398298 3. All rows and all columns should be unique.
%C A398298 Only the first two rules impose a constraint on this sequence.
%C A398298 Each row n of the triangle corresponds to a 2*n X 2*n Takuzu board.
%C A398298 The scatter plot of this sequence appears to be self-similar: every time all terms for a board are added, the scatter plot retains its shape and only contains more datapoints.
%H A398298 Stef Bordo, <a href="/A398298/b398298.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..13612</a> (first 10 rows)
%H A398298 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Takuzu">Takuzu</a>.
%e A398298 For a 2 X 2 board, only the first rule applies and the valid rows are 01 and 10 (1 and 2 in decimal).
%e A398298 For a 4 X 4 board, the valid rows are 0011 (3), 0101 (5), 0110 (6), 1001 (9), 1010 (10) and 1100 (12).
%e A398298 For a 6 X 6 board, the valid rows are 001011 (11), 001101 (13), 010011 (19) and so forth.
%e A398298 These are arranged as an irregular triangle, with the valid numbers for a 2*n X 2*n board on the n-th row:
%e A398298   1, 2;
%e A398298   3, 5, 6, 9, 10, 12;
%e A398298   11, 13, 19, 21, 22, 25, 26, 37, 38, 41, 42, 44, 50, 52;
%e A398298   ...
%o A398298 (Python)
%o A398298 def generate_takuzu(max_board_size):
%o A398298     for n in range(1, max_board_size//2+1):
%o A398298         N = 2**(2*n)
%o A398298         for i in range(N//7, N):
%o A398298             bin_str = f"{i:0{2*n}b}"
%o A398298             count_check = bin_str.count("0") == bin_str.count("1")
%o A398298             repeat_check = "000" not in bin_str and "111" not in bin_str
%o A398298             if count_check and repeat_check:
%o A398298                 yield i
%Y A398298 Subsequence of A072601 and A063037.
%Y A398298 Cf. A177790 (number of terms in row n), A253316.
%K A398298 nonn,easy,tabf,new
%O A398298 1,2
